From c72b6db919fb94f29b06224950126917f44e646f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 11:12:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Keep the television depth test on at every present, with
 frame generation

In game, with frame generation on, the picture still showed over V's body and
hands at most presents, and the game stuttered. The log said why: about four
presents for every frame the game rendered (10533 of 12478 presents in 30 s
had no depth work before them), and the chosen buffer changing at almost every
present between the scene's (853x533 D32_S8, ~2400 draws) and a small pass's of
the same size (D32, 119 draws).

Three things followed from judging one present at a time:

  * The depth test was applied only at a present that had just copied the
    depth; at the others the picture was drawn with the test off, over
    everything. With four presents a frame, that was most of them.
  * Whichever buffer had been drawn into since the last present was taken for
    the scene's, so the copy alternated between the scene and the small pass.
  * Each change of buffer changed the copy's format, so the copy texture was
    made again (and logged) at almost every present: the stutter.

Now:

  * The choice is made on each buffer's work summed over the last several
    presents, shrinking by an eighth a present (SceneDepthPolicy::History).
    The scene's buffer stays chosen whether or not this present drew into it;
    another takes over only by doing more than twice its work, as before.
  * The overlay's copy is used at every present until a newer one replaces it,
    for at most 30 presents, so the presents between the game's frames keep
    the test.
  * The chosen buffer is copied once its frame's depth is finished: only if the
    game used it since the last present (one it has let go of may be gone),
    changed it since the last copy, and moved it into a read-only state since
    its last clear or left it in one (SceneDepthPolicy::ShouldCopy). Clears are
    recorded in order with the state changes, so "since its last clear" means
    what it does on the GPU. A frame whose work spans presents is no longer
    copied half drawn; a buffer the game never reads is copied whenever it
    changes, and one found mid-frame eight times since the last copy is copied
    anyway.
  * The calibration samples only at presents that copied, where the screen's
    corners and the depth are of the same moment.
  * A change of buffer is logged at most every five seconds; the status line
    counts them all, and now the presents that waited for a finished frame.
  * Copies go on for 600 presents after a screen was last drawn (was 120), so
    a screen turned back to is tested from its first present.

Tested: the policy tests pin the history on the game's own pattern (the
scene's buffer chosen at every present from the first, a new buffer taking
over within four frames, gone ones forgotten) and the copy timing. The
end-to-end run on Wine's Direct3D 12 (outside the repository) gains a frame
generation mode: one game frame per two to four presents, its work spread over
them, a small pass's buffer of the scene's size in between. With the previous
code 21 of 27 presents drew the picture over the hand and the small buffer was
chosen; now every present once the depth is measured shows the picture behind
the hand and nowhere else hidden, in all four variants, and the four earlier
variants still pass.

